W: 2. Which caused his master for to grieve,
W: Likewise his mistress too,
W: Until their sorrows to relieve,
W: They sampled of the brew.
W: "Odzoons!" cried Farmer Atwater,
W: "The like I ne'er did sup,
W: Go summon all the neighbors in,
W: And bid them take a cup."
W:
W: 3. So the neighbors came from far and near,
W: The parson and the squire,
W: The blacksmith and the gamekeeper,
W: And daft old [Obediah ?],
W: They wished the farmer health and wealth,
W: And the parson played his fife,
W: And the squire, he filled three flagons up,
W: For to take home to his wife.
W:
W: 4. Now every man that drank that night,
W: Got drunk as drunk could be,
W: And they wondered how the scrumpy had,
W: Acquired such potency.
W: But the farmer kept his counsel,
W: As he took another drop,
W: When all at once the poor old dog,
W: Came floating to the top.
W:
W: 5. Now a silence fell upon the room,
W: And every man did frown,
W: They recognized old Bendigo,
W: Though he were upside-down.
W: The vicar lost his color,
W: And collapsed upon the floor,
W: The squire he lost his britches,
W: In a rush to reach the door.
W:
W: 6. "Fear not," said Farmer Atwater,
W: "For in all his life I vow,
W: He never bit no man nor dog,
W: And he'll not bite no one now.
W: And this shall be his epitaph:
W: 'Here lies our faithful Ben,
W: What perished in a scrumpy vat,
W: And quickly rose again.'"
W:
W: 7. So if you're down to Devon,
W: And you goes into a bar,
W: Just ask for Dead Dog Scrumpy,
W: 'T'is the best there is by far.
W: Refuse all imitations and,
W: You'll sleep just like a log,
W: You can always recognize it,
W: By the hair of the dog.
